[#:seon.task{:name "a-pending-put-or-take-is-a-waiter-not-a-thread",
             :plan
             "Reproduction (flowlab/e0_put_threads.clj): from 4 threads, 10 pending put! -> 14 threads, 100 -> 104; 100 pending take! -> 105; an unbounded 100,000 put! loop reached ~7,500 threads and a machine load of 982. Cause: host/chez/java/async.ss:900-919, `jolt-async-put!` and `jolt-async-take!` fork a daemon thread that blocks in jolt-async-give/take whenever the op cannot complete at once. Everything built on them inherits it: mult (async.clj:891-900, put! per tap per value), pub/sub, mix, pipe, onto-chan, and core.async.flow's fan-out mults (flow/impl.clj routes-for). Correct: a pending put!/take! registers a callback waiter in the existing alt-handler protocol (alt-putters/alt-takers, async.ss:33-54, alt-deliver! 300-311), whose wake runs the callback on a fiber (fibers-async.ss's waiter, a third wake strategy beside the condvar and the fiber resume). No cap: a pending op costs a handler record. Replaces the per-op daemon thread.",
             :kind :work,
             :proofs
             #{jolt.async-test/a-mult-with-a-full-tap-costs-no-thread
               jolt.async-test/a-pending-take-costs-no-thread
               jolt.async-test/a-pending-put-costs-no-thread}}
