 {:seon.task/name "a-process-whose-every-thread-parks-ends-its-waits"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the stability sweep of 2026-10-25 (notes/archive/stability-2026-10-25.md; the owner: platform stability first). The field's way (Go's runtime: 'all goroutines are asleep - deadlock!'): a process in which every thread and fiber is parked on a channel, promise or mutex, with no timer pending, no thread in a foreign call and no I/O wait registered with the poller, can never wake itself. Today it parks forever: the test runner's per-test tick budget (seon.proofs/test-vars, :seon.config.agent/test-ticks) counts work, and a parked test does none, so nothing ends it (the full run's shard 3: 61 min; agent_test's await-quiet: 1h45m). Correct, in Jolt (jolt/<topic>, root's go first: a dependency change): the scheduler detects global quiescence of the process and raises a typed error {:jolt/deadlock true :parked [what each thread waits on]} in each parked deref or <!! of the main carrier's waiters, which unwinds to its caller: in a shard, the test, which reports it as an :error with the parked stacks as its cause, and the shard goes on. A process waiting on a child's socket is in an I/O wait and is not quiescent; the child ends its own waits (seon.root/a-wait-on-main-ends-when-nothing-can-make-it-true) or exits (EOF), so the chain unwinds from the bottom. No clock. Proofs: a test that derefs an undelivered promise reports :error naming the promise and the next test runs; a test waiting on a live child's socket is not touched."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-main-carrier-is-stuck-only-when-no-thread-runs"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the deadlock design (notes/archive/jolt-deadlock-design-2026-10-25.md): jolt.host/main-carrier-run! raises stuck (host/chez/fibers.ss ~1676) when no fiber is queued, no timer set and no descriptor awaited, ignoring the OS threads a future or a Thread. still runs, so a fiber waiting on a working future is declared stuck. Seon's agents and one-thread test shards run in that mode. Correct, in Jolt (jolt/<topic>): count the OS threads the runtime forks (%ls-fork-thread, host/chez/lazy-bridge.ss ~342, where each is born and dies) and declare stuck only when none is running beside the main carrier. Proofs (Jolt): a fiber waiting on a working future is not stuck and gets its value; a fiber with nothing that can wake it is still stuck."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-adapter-runtimes-buffers-are-freed-at-the-real-exit"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the audit of host/chez's dynamic-winds (Jolt jolt/a-read-scope-holds-across-a-park dfdc33ef: every winder whose cleanup closes or frees what its body uses takes jolt-finally-in, which a park or preemption drops, so the cleanup runs at the real exit; a plain winder runs at the park, and the resumed body meets a closed port or freed memory, as a fork server's zipdir-read did at boot). Two are left, in host/chez/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss: its buffer winder near line 555 (foreign-free buf; its before-thunk zeroes the buffer, so it also runs again on every rewind) and the one near line 666 (the Windows environment block, free block). That file loads before values.ss defines jolt-finally-in (fibers.ss captures the marker by guarded reference for the same reason), so the marker cannot be named there as the others are: the setup moves out of the before-thunk, and the cleanup either takes the marker by the same guarded, late-bound reference or moves into a file loaded after values.ss. Proof: a preemption stepped through each body (the scheduler's quantum, as zip-index-preempt-test does for zipdir-read) answers the body's value, and the buffer is freed once at the end."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-threaded-process-whose-every-thread-parks-ends-its-waits"
  :seon.task/plan "Slice 2 of task jolt.async/a-process-whose-every-thread-parks-ends-its-waits (slice 1: main-carrier mode, jolt branch jolt/a-process-whose-every-thread-parks-ends-its-waits: when no fiber is queued, no timer set, no descriptor awaited and every other live thread is in an untimed jolt wait, fibers.ss's main loop tells each parked fiber once, by {:jolt/deadlock true :parked dump} thrown in its own frame). Threaded mode is where the hangs were (bin/test shards, test/forks/run.jolt, the forks-bggc pid 70576: the main thread in a jolt cv wait, the carriers idle in their condition waits, 18 fibers parked on alt-handlers; nothing detects anything). Design (design note side-session-notes/.../all-asleep-design-2026-10-27.md): an event-driven awake count, no clock: each jolt thread entering an untimed jolt wait (locks.ss jolt-condition-wait, a carrier's idle wait) counts down, each wake counts up; the thread that takes it to zero checks timers (async.ss timeout-pending-count), the poller (io_poller.clj awaited?) and threads outside jolt waits (foreign calls, raw waits: counted awake, so a deadlock with one is missed, never invented), and if all asleep tells every waiter: fibers by jolt-fiber-deliver-interrupt!, and a plain thread's cv wait needs an interrupt path of its own (verify what jolt's Thread.interrupt reaches). Jolt's own plumbing threads record their raw waits (slice 1 did the shutdown watcher's sigwait with call-with-thread-state 'WAITING; the process reaper and tap-loop to check). Proofs red first: in a plain (non-main-carrier) process, a test that derefs an undelivered promise reports :error naming the promise and the next test runs; a future that delivers after a sleep is not touched; the forks-bggc shape (a go block dies, the main thread waits on its value) ends with the error. Review by the jolt session before merging (it touches every wait path). Slice 1's known miss, which this slice covers: slice 1 counts a thread in TIMED_WAITING as one that may run, so the loop waits on carrier 0's condition; a thread that wakes from a timed wait and enters an untimed one (a future that sleeps, then derefs an undelivered promise) neither enqueues nor ends, so the deadlock is missed (no worse than before slice 1). Here every untimed-wait entry counts down and wakes the check. Proof: that shape ends with the error. Done in jolt dd517d0e and its review fix (8efcd439 the threaded detector; dd517d0e the main loop woken by the asleep hint): one mechanism in Jolt's own wait paths, six hook sites (jolt-condition-wait, jolt-cv-wake!/signal-one!, a thread's end, the shutdown watcher's sigwait, the poller's idle round, the fork-child reset), nothing in Seon. Stated: in main-carrier mode the loop tells fibers only (jolt-main-carrier-deliver-deadlock!), so a plain thread in a value wait (a future derefing a promise no one delivers) stays parked; the main fiber's unwind ends the program, the thread with it. Threaded mode tells both."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"jolt.async/a-process-whose-every-thread-parks-ends-its-waits"}}
